## Snowpark DataFrame API

### Creating DataFrames

```python
# From a table
table_name = 'DATABASE.SCHEMA.TABLE_NAME'
df = session.table(table_name)

# From SQL query - ALWAYS format the query string first, then pass it to session.sql()
query = f"""
SELECT * FROM DATABASE.SCHEMA.TABLE_NAME
WHERE "id" > 10
"""
df = session.sql(query)

# From SQL query with dynamic variables
cutoff_date = (datetime.utcnow() - timedelta(days=90)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d')

query = f"""
SELECT COUNT(*) AS active_user_count
FROM users
WHERE last_used_date >= '{cutoff_date}'
"""
result = session.sql(query).to_pandas()

# From Python data
df = session.create_dataframe([[1, 'a'], [2, 'b']], schema=['id', 'name'])

# With explicit schema
from snowflake.snowpark.types import StructType, StructField, IntegerType, StringType
schema = StructType([StructField("id", IntegerType()), StructField("name", StringType())])
df = session.create_dataframe([[1, "a"]], schema)
```

### Lazy Evaluation - Action Methods

DataFrames are lazy. Call these methods to execute:

| Method | Returns | Use Case |
|--------|---------|----------|
| `df.collect()` | List of Row objects | When you need all data as Python objects |
| `df.to_pandas()` | pandas DataFrame | For pandas operations or visualizations |
| `df.show(n)` | None (prints to console) | Debugging only |
| `df.count()` | Integer | Get row count |

### Transformations

```python
from snowflake.snowpark.functions import col, lit, sum, avg, count, when

# Filter rows
df.filter(col("id") > 10)
df.filter((col("status") == "active") & (col("value") > 100))
    DO NOT USE `df.filter(col("DATE_COL") >= dateadd("day", -90, current_date()) )`  # INVALID SYNTAX
    USE `df.filter(col("DATE_COL") >= datetime.utcnow() - timedelta(days=90))` INSTEAD


# Select columns
df.select(col("id"), col("name"), col("value"))
df.select("id", "name")  # String shorthand

# Add/rename columns
df.with_column("new_col", col("value") * 2)
df.with_column_renamed("old_name", "new_name")
df.select(col("name").alias("customer_name"))

# Aggregations
df.group_by("category").agg(
    sum("amount").alias("total"),
    avg("price").alias("avg_price"),
    count("*").alias("count")
)

# Sorting
df.sort(col("date").desc())
df.order_by(col("name").asc(), col("id").desc())

# Limit
df.limit(100)
```

### Column References

```python
from snowflake.snowpark.functions import col

# All equivalent:
col("column_name")      # Using col function (recommended)
df["column_name"]       # Bracket notation
df.column_name          # Dot notation (only for simple names)
```

### Joining DataFrames

```python
# Inner join
df1.join(df2, df1["id"] == df2["id"])

# Join on same column name
df1.join(df2, ["id"])

# Join with suffix for overlapping columns
df1.join(df2, df1["id"] == df2["id"], lsuffix="_left", rsuffix="_right")

# Self-join (must copy first)
from copy import copy
df_copy = copy(df)
df.join(df_copy, df["id"] == df_copy["parent_id"])
```

### Working with Semi-Structured Data (JSON/VARIANT)

```python
# Access nested fields
df.select(col("src")["field_name"])
df.select(col("src")["nested"]["field"])

# Access array elements
df.select(col("src")["items"][0])

# Cast to specific type
from snowflake.snowpark.types import StringType
df.select(col("src")["name"].cast(StringType()))

# Flatten arrays
df.join_table_function("flatten", col("src")["items"])
```

### Type Casting

```python
from snowflake.snowpark.types import IntegerType, StringType, DecimalType

df.select(col("value").cast(IntegerType()))
df.select(col("price").cast(DecimalType(10, 2)))
```

